title: Moving off NixOS and Docker means installing the tools by hand; one script calls Docker, and nothing else depends on it.
pid: 49d94d7f-423f-4835-a238-2c9e891d697b
body: |2-
  The container's image, built with Nix outside this repository,
  supplies everything: Rust 1.99 (the code needs edition 2024),
  cargo-nextest and cargo-watch, gcc, cmake and pkg-config for the C
  libraries cargo compiles (libgit2, zlib, zstd), Emacs 31 with magit,
  Neovim 0.12 with nvim-orgmode, its tree-sitter parser, plenary, neogit
  and diffview, Python 3, Node for Claude Code, and git, ripgrep, fd and
  jq. On a plain Linux these come from the package manager, rustup and
  Neovim's plugin manager; the Neovim parser is the fiddly one.
  What names Docker: 'bash/import-org-roam.sh' runs its import with
  'docker exec'; the 'windows/' folder packages skg for Windows users
  in Docker, a separate product; and 'coding-advice/docker.org' tells
  Claude it is in a container. The image also points Claude's config
  at a folder in the repository, so sessions survive a rebuild; without
  it the team's tools fall back to '~/.claude' and still work.
  Nothing in the server, the clients or the tests assumes either.
  If you do not answer, nothing moves.
